ly 12: parent of P17). The aunt and two nieces are given as relatives of P4's grandfather and the cousin's relation is unspecified ('another cousin'), so those labels chain to the grandfather: an interpretation. Supplementary figures 1 and 2 are converted in a scrambled layout with no patient labels, so nothing was attributed from them. CONTRADICTIONS and inconsistencies: (1) the text says P7 received baricitinib 'with partial clinical improvement' while supplementary table 1c prints effectiveness 'no' for P7; (2) the text reports 'mildly elevated faecal calprotectin' for P1 while table 1b prints 'neg'; (3) positive ANA is counted as 7/13 in the text but 8 of the 14 printed cells are '+'; (4) hypergammaglobulinaemia '4/11' in the text vs 3 printed 'yes'; (5) elevated IgA '3/11' vs 4 printed 'yes'; (6) IgG '5/10' vs 5 'yes' of 12 tested; (7) table 1 gives median age at diagnosis 13.5 years vs 13 years in the text. PHENOTYPES: features that map onto a checklist item are recorded in phenotype_checklist only, with the paper's own wording as the span; phenotypic_features holds the features that are not checklist items. The onset and disease-course blocks of supplementary table 1a were merged per feature (observed if Present in either block, excluded only if Absent in both), and the block supporting the status is quoted, so for P6 and P7 fever (Present at onset, Absent in the course row) the status is observed and the course cell is not recorded. MAPPINGS I made: 'Fever' to 'recurrent fever'; 'Skin eruption' to 'skin lesions (rash, folliculitis, erythema nodosum)'; 'autoimmune diabetes' (P10) to 'type 1 diabetes'; colitis, P1's aphthoid colonic ulcerations and P8's gastritis to 'gastrointestinal ulcers or inflammation'; the paper's neurological symptom (headache) to 'central nervous system involvement'. Lymphadenopathy alone was not used to exclude the composite 'lymphoproliferation or lymphadenopathy', nor pericarditis to exclude 'pericarditis or other serositis', nor 'no arthritis' or 'no arthralgia' singly to exclude 'arthritis or arthralgia'. 'elevated inflammatory markers (CRP/ESR)' is 'not reported' for everyone: CRP and ESR are printed only as numbers with no per-person reference range and the only 'elevated' statement is cohort-level. Group statements that name the positive patients (autoimmune thyroiditis, autoimmune diabetes, autoimmune hepatitis, neurological symptoms, ocular involvement) were used to mark the other patients 'excluded' with evidence_basis 'inferred from group statement'. 'autoantibodies (ANA/other)' is observed only where a printed antibody cell is '+' (read through the table 1b legend) or the text names one (P7 anti-TPO, P8 gastric autoantibodies); the other table 1b binary and numeric rows (AST/ALT, hypergammaglobulinaemia, hyper IgA/IgM/IgG, rheumatoid factor, dsDNA, HLA-B51, interferon signature, faecal calprotectin, Hb/WBC/CRP/ESR/SAA) were read but not emitted as individual phenotypic_features rows. HPO candidates are null because no identifier could be verified from the allowed sources. No stacked ' / ' cell was used for any person: they occur only in the flare rows of supplementary table 1a and in supplementary table 2, a disease-comparison table with no patients. Age ISO values keep the printed decimals (P0.3Y for '0.3'). Ethnicity, age at last evaluation and vital status are not reported per person: the paper gives only cohort figures, and the figure captions' ages (P6 14 years, P7 15 years) are ages at imaging, not last evaluation. P1 and P16 were first labelled PFAPA, noted here rather than in disease_label_as_stated. treatment_summary spans are capped at five, so a long regimen is not quoted in full.",
